#{[17592186139263 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186139264 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186139263 #edb/ref 1061 131583 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186139263 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "feedfa63-390b-53d3-b4e9-ba9fa9628476" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186139263 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186138368 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186139264 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186139265 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186139264 #edb/ref 1061 131586 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186139264 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "56aaa78e-43e0-50c6-a007-8f7f41855d42" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186139264 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186138369 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186139265 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186139266 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186139265 #edb/ref 1061 131589 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186139265 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "a039ef0e-db7b-520c-acb5-321b992f0f47" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186139265 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186139711 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186139266 #edb/ref 1061 131590 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186139266 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "405d6b9e-8686-5904-853f-89c53abda3d6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186139266 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186138370 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186139709 #edb/ref 1064 "Suppose we want to compute the sum of two numbers with different numbers of decimal places, such as\n\n$$\n19+5.6\n$$\n\nHere, there are no decimal places in $19$, but there is $\\color{blue}1$ decimal place in $5.6$.\n\nIn order to add the two numbers, we need the two numbers to have the same number of decimal places. We can achieve this by attaching an extra zero behind the decimal place in the first number, so that it has $\\color{blue}1$ decimal place:\n\n$$\n1\n$$\n\nRemember that whenever we attach an extra zero behind the decimal place, the number remains unchanged. So the number $19.0$ is equivalent to the original number $19$.\n\nNow the addition problem reads\n\n$$\n19.0 + 5.6\n$$\n\nand we can add the decimal numbers using the usual method. First, we line up the decimal points:\n\n$$\n1\\begin{vmatrix}9 &. & 0 \\\\ + & 5 &.\\end{vmatrix}6;\n$$\n\nThen, we proceed by adding the two numbers just as we would with whole numbers:\n\n$$\n1; 1\\begin{vmatrix}9 &. & 0 \\\\ + & 5 &.\\end{vmatrix}6; 2\\begin{vmatrix}4 &.\\end{vmatrix}6\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n19 + 5.6 = 24.6\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186139709 #edb/ref 1065 8659 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186139709 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "bbd5cc8c-5c61-557c-ac3d-b35131923855" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186139709 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186139711 #edb/ref 1064 "Sometimes, we will need to place more than one zero to the right of a decimal point.\n\nFor instance, to find the value of $78.9 + 45.962$, notice that there is $\\color{blue}1$ decimal place in $78.9$ and there are $\\color{blue}3$ decimal places in $45.962$.\n\nTo add the two numbers, we need them to have the same number of decimal places. So, we rewrite the first number so that it has $\\color{blue}3$ decimal places:\n\n$$\n78.9=78.9{\\color{red}00}\n$$\n\nNext, we line up the decimal points:\n\n$$\n7\\begin{vmatrix}8 &. & 9 & 0 & 0 \\\\ + & 4 & 5 &. & 9\\end{vmatrix}6 \\mid 2;\n$$\n\nFinally, we proceed by adding the two numbers just as we would with whole numbers:\n\n$$\n1\\begin{vmatrix}1 \\\\ 7\\end{vmatrix}8\\begin{vmatrix}. & 9 & 0 & 0 \\\\ + & 4 & 5 &.\\end{vmatrix}9\\begin{vmatrix}6 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{vmatrix}4\\begin{vmatrix}. & 8 & 6\\end{vmatrix}2\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n78.9 + 45.962 = 124.862\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186139711 #edb/ref 1065 8662 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186139711 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "b92b614c-df34-57df-b4f4-f8d2a4585e5d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186139711 #edb/ref 1067 "Decimal Addition With Multiple Decimal Place Differences" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186147415 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186049253 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186147415 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186050170 #edb/ref 17592186045